WEBVTT

00:00:00.550 --> 00:00:02.245
Lesson 10.3.

00:00:02.245 --> 00:00:04.940
Delta-neutral funding carry, done properly.

00:00:04.940 --> 00:00:07.116
By the end of this lesson, you'll be

00:00:07.116 --> 00:00:09.998
able to run a funding-carry position with proper

00:00:09.998 --> 00:00:12.115
sizing, venue limits and exit rules.

00:00:13.415 --> 00:00:14.705
Here's the plan.

00:00:14.705 --> 00:00:16.390
First, the key ideas.

00:00:16.390 --> 00:00:19.105
Then, a worked example, step by step.

00:00:19.105 --> 00:00:20.630
Then, your checklist.

00:00:20.630 --> 00:00:22.792
And finally, a three-question quiz.

00:00:24.092 --> 00:00:27.063
Perpetual futures pay funding between longs and

00:00:27.063 --> 00:00:29.467
shorts to keep the perp near spot.

00:00:29.467 --> 00:00:32.597
In bullish markets longs usually pay shorts.

00:00:32.597 --> 00:00:35.384
Long spot + short perp at the same size

00:00:35.384 --> 00:00:39.167
= no net price exposure, while collecting funding.

00:00:39.167 --> 00:00:43.547
Professional rules: First, Measure funding over time, not today.

00:00:43.547 --> 00:00:46.002
Use a 7- and 30-day average.

00:00:46.002 --> 00:00:48.442
Next, Exit rule: close when the 7-day

00:00:48.442 --> 00:00:51.567
average turns negative (you'd start paying).

00:00:51.567 --> 00:00:54.484
Then, Margin buffer: the short leg's leverage

00:00:54.484 --> 00:00:57.067
decides how big a rally it survives.

00:00:57.067 --> 00:00:59.093
And finally, Venue limits: split

00:00:59.093 --> 00:01:00.812
across venues; cap each one.

00:01:02.113 --> 00:01:06.439
Funding +0.01% per 8h ≈ 10.95%

00:01:06.439 --> 00:01:09.948
APR on the hedged size.

00:01:09.948 --> 00:01:16.578
the calculator Higher leverage improves return on capital a

00:01:16.578 --> 00:01:21.323
little and makes the position much

00:01:21.323 --> 00:01:27.698
more fragile. Most operators stay at 1–2× and top

00:01:27.698 --> 00:01:33.708
up margin from reserve. 1×, $2.00, ~5.5%, +100%.

00:01:33.708 --> 00:01:39.273
2×, $1.50, ~7.3%, +50%. 3×, $1.33, ~8.2%, +33%.

00:01:40.573 --> 00:01:43.938
Funding turning negative, short squeezes, and venue failure.

00:01:45.238 --> 00:01:46.698
Here's your checklist.

00:01:46.698 --> 00:01:48.233
Do it now, for real.

00:01:48.233 --> 00:01:51.677
First, 7- and 30-day funding averages checked.

00:01:51.677 --> 00:01:55.982
Next, Exit rule written (e.g. 7-day average < 0).

00:01:55.982 --> 00:02:00.697
Then, Short-leg leverage ≤ 2×, top-up plan from reserve.

00:02:00.697 --> 00:02:02.642
And finally, Per-venue cap set.

00:02:03.941 --> 00:02:05.151
Question 1.

00:02:05.151 --> 00:02:09.576
Funding is +0.005%/8h.

00:02:09.576 --> 00:02:11.411
APR on hedged size?

00:02:15.831 --> 00:02:18.376
The answer: ≈ 5.5%.

00:02:19.676 --> 00:02:20.921
Question 2.

00:02:20.921 --> 00:02:22.846
Why not run the short at 5×?

00:02:27.266 --> 00:02:30.014
The answer: A ~20% rally would liquidate

00:02:30.014 --> 00:02:32.561
the short and leave you unhedged long.

00:02:33.861 --> 00:02:35.131
Question 3.

00:02:35.131 --> 00:02:36.491
What's the exit signal?

00:02:40.911 --> 00:02:43.008
The answer: Average funding turning

00:02:43.008 --> 00:02:45.221
negative, meaning shorts start paying.

00:02:46.521 --> 00:02:47.766
Let's recap.

00:02:47.766 --> 00:02:50.401
Measure funding over time, not today.

00:02:50.401 --> 00:02:52.418
Exit rule: close when the 7-day

00:02:52.418 --> 00:02:55.491
average turns negative (you'd start paying).

00:02:55.491 --> 00:02:58.469
Margin buffer: the short leg's leverage decides

00:02:58.469 --> 00:03:00.506
how big a rally it survives.

00:03:00.506 --> 00:03:02.701
Venue limits: split across venues.

00:03:04.001 --> 00:03:06.516
Do the checklist now, before you move on.

00:03:06.516 --> 00:03:09.362
Next up, Lesson 10.4: Options income:

00:03:09.362 --> 00:03:13.031
covered calls, cash-secured puts, options vaults.
